{"from": "claim:lk-09-07-herod-perplexed", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-14-02-herod-thought-john-risen", "note": "Matthew's Herod tells his servants Jesus is John risen; Luke has Herod perplexed between John-risen, Elijah and an old prophet."}
{"from": "claim:lk-09-10-city-called-bethsaida", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-14-13-withdrew-by-boat", "note": "Luke names Bethsaida as the destination of the withdrawal; Matthew says only a deserted place. Mark 6:45's Bethsaida is the post-feeding boat destination, so Mark's Bethsaida mention comes after the feeding, Luke's before it."}
{"from": "claim:lk-09-10-city-called-bethsaida", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:luke-9-10", "note": "The Luke 9:10 text node is the biblical cross-reference carried by the graph since Matthew 14."}
{"from": "claim:lk-09-18-question", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-16-13-son-of-man-question", "note": "Same question; Luke omits the Caesarea-Philippi setting and places it while Jesus is praying alone."}
{"from": "claim:lk-09-19-identifications", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-16-14-popular-identifications", "note": "Luke omits Jeremiah and says 'one of the old prophets is risen again'."}
{"from": "claim:lk-09-20-christ-of-god", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-16-16-peters-confession", "note": "Luke: 'The Christ of God'; Matthew: 'the Christ, the Son of the living God'; Mark: 'the Christ'."}
{"from": "claim:lk-09-52-53-samaritan-village-refused", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-10-05-samaritan-people", "note": "The Samaritans as a distinct people centred on Gerizim are the background of the village's refusal."}
{"from": "claim:lk-09-54-55-fire-rebuked", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:2-kings-1-10-12", "note": "Elijah's fire from heaven on the captains of fifty is the scriptural model James and John invoke (biblical cross-reference, not evidence)."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-cumanus", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikisource-ant20-cumanus"}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-cumanus", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikisource-ant20-ginea"}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-cumanus", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-09-52-53-samaritan-village-refused", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus: Galilean pilgrims 'take their journeys through the country of the Samaritans' to the festivals, and a village on that road (Ginea, in the limits of Samaria) killed many of them under Cumanus (c. 48 CE) - the same route, period and Samaritan-Galilean hostility Luke's refused village presupposes; it does not name Luke's village or occasion."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-cumanus", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-09-51-set-face-to-jerusalem", "grade": "C", "note": "The same passage attests the standing custom of Galilean pilgrimage through Samaria toward 'the holy city' at the festivals - the journey Luke's narrative now turns toward."}
{"from": "text:2-kings-1-10-12", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:bible-api-2-kings-1-12"}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-bethsaida", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-09-10-city-called-bethsaida", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus names 'the village Bethsaids, situate at the lake of Gennesareth', advanced by Philip to the dignity of a city and renamed Julias - the named city Luke sets the withdrawal near (reused from Matthew 11/14; which modern site it was is disputed separately). Regraded A to B by architect review: a grade covers the whole claim; Josephus confirms Bethsaida as a city in Philip's day (the setting, including Luke's word 'city'), not the withdrawal."}
{"from": "site:et-tell", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-09-10-city-called-bethsaida", "grade": "D", "note": "et-Tell is the long-standing candidate for Bethsaida, 'inhabited on a lesser scale during the first centuries BCE and CE'; the identification is now disputed by el-Araj (reused site and sources)."}
{"from": "site:el-araj", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:lk-09-10-city-called-bethsaida", "grade": "D", "note": "el-Araj's excavators report Roman-layer pottery, coins and stone vessels 'typical of first-century Jewish life' on the north shore, arguing it 'a much better candidate for Bethsaida than et-Tell'; the identification is disputed (reused site and sources)."}
